  <h2>Separate identities for parents and administrators</h2>

  <p>
    Entra External ID supports self-service parent registration. Administrative privilege comes
    from a separate workforce app registration, rather than a flag in the Cosmos container that an
    administrator edits. The privilege and the application data therefore have separate control
    planes and bootstrap paths.
  </p>

  <p>
    The administrator's token carries the privilege in its <code>roles</code> claim. Parent and admin
    applications have different audiences, and the API checks the audience before application
    logic. Only a workforce token reaches the app-role check on administrative routes.
  </p>

  <h2>Household isolation</h2>

  <p>Parent routes resolve the household through the account associated with the validated subject. Portal routes resolve it through the invited membership and limit photograph access to that member's submissions. Device routes compare the key with the digest configured for the household in the URL; changing that URL cannot authorize another family.</p>
  <p>Household records in Cosmos DB use family partitioning, and image archives use household paths. Invitations and memberships currently share one private Blob document with conditional writes and application-level access checks. That register is not physically partitioned by family. Adding a home requires provisioning a distinct key and its server-side household binding.</p>

  <p>The code enforces household boundaries, but the system has only run in one home. Provisioning and operation across multiple households remain untested.</p>

  <h2>House authentication</h2>

  <p>
    The hub presents its device key in the <code>X-Device-Key</code> header over TLS. The API holds
    the Azure credential and performs model calls for page reading, reminder interpretation and
    activity continuation. Device routes return 503 until household key bindings are configured.
  </p>

  <p>
    A hub therefore carries a credential for its household routes rather than a credential that can
    be replayed against the Azure subscription. This boundary makes cloud connectivity a requirement
    for page reading.
  </p>

  <p>The API stores SHA-256 digests of random hub keys. Unknown households, missing keys and incorrect keys receive HTTP 403. Rotation replaces the digest; revocation removes the binding. A stolen key remains usable within its authorized household until revocation takes effect. This bearer-key scheme does not provide device attestation or mutual TLS.</p>

  <h2>Invited adolescent access</h2>
  <p>The parent creates an email-bound invitation and shares its code or link. The adolescent signs in with the matching address and accepts the invitation. Registration alone grants no portal access. An active membership permits submitting, listing and deleting that member's photographs, not approving activities or changing household settings. Revocation closes subsequent portal requests. Known adolescent subjects, including revoked memberships, are excluded from the parent gate. Suspension of the inviting parent also closes portal access.</p>
  <p>The portal uses the existing External ID application and audience. Its permissions come from the server-side membership, not from a separate adolescent token audience. First registration and redemption with a real invited account remain a family acceptance check.</p>

  <h2>Application boundaries</h2>
  <p>The route groups share a process, deployment and runtime identity. This keeps deployment and shared data access simple, but a process compromise or resource exhaustion can affect all groups. Moving administration to a separate service would provide an additional boundary only with independently restricted runtime permissions and access controls; another hostname alone would not provide that isolation.</p>

  <h2>Activity approval</h2>

  <p>
    The panel stores parent decisions. The hub retrieves approved activities through authenticated HTTPS and chooses when to begin. Continuations are checked and screened in the container but receive no second parent approval.
  </p>

  <h2>OIDC deployment</h2>

  <p>
    GitHub Actions exchanges its OIDC token for an Azure token through a federated credential on a
    user-assigned managed identity. Three resource-scoped role assignments allow the pipeline to
    push to one registry, read that registry and update one container app.
  </p>

  <h2>Known authorization gaps</h2>

  <p>
    The token validator checks audience, issuer, expiry and subject but omits <code>scp</code>. The
    current app exposes one scope. Adding another scope would require a scope check before it could
    separate permissions.
  </p>

